pySMESH: netgen writes to its own message streams, never straight to the console
(netgen 6.2.2101; applies after occgeom_save_without_stl.patch).

Why: netgen has two message streams of its own, netgen::mycout and netgen::myerr
(general/template.hpp; meshing/global.cpp sets them to &cout and &cerr), which
PrintMessage, PrintWarning and PrintError use and which an embedding program can point
elsewhere. Many netgen sources write past them, straight to std::cout and std::cerr, and
pySMESH is embedded in a host program whose console it must leave alone. Measured with a
capture of fd 1 and fd 2 on the NETGEN algorithms
(docs/reports/agents/phase5_netgen/g2/np3_output_scan.py and np3_failure_timing.py):
"retry Surface 1" on every sphere, the "WARNING! NOT ALL FACES HAVE BEEN MESHED" block on a
failed face and on a cancel (occ/occgenmesh.cpp), "el1 = ...", "el2 = ...",
"layer1 = ...", "layer2 = ..." for each pair of intersecting surface triangles (184 blocks
on one sphere; meshing/meshclass.cpp), and "meshed area = ...", "maximal area = ...",
"GIVING UP" (meshing/meshing2.cpp). A test reaches only some paths; the others are the
same kind of message on the same meshing code.

Change, made by a script (docs/reports/agents/phase5_netgen/scripts/
netgen_console_rewrite.py) in the code, not in comments or string literals, of every file
pySMESH compiles or includes from libsrc/{meshing,occ,general,linalg,gprim,stlgeom,include}
and of nglib/nglib.cpp: std::cout / cout becomes (*::netgen::mycout), and std::cerr / cerr /
clog becomes (*::netgen::myerr). The lines that define or assign the two streams
(meshing/global.cpp, Ng_Init) do not change. Run on its own, netgen prints exactly what
it printed before; inside pySMESH, NETGENPlugin_runtime_containment.patch points both
streams at a stream with no buffer. libsrc/interface, csg and geom2d (file formats and
geometry kinds pySMESH never calls) and ngcore (its logger is switched off; the rest is
GCC-only or unused) are left as they are. No mesh changes: only where the text goes.

